{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_terminal_parisi_assembly","record":{"id":"scalar.source_terminal_parisi_assembly","kind":"claim","label":"Terminal formulas at the Parisi value after concrete suppliers","status":"unverified","source_id":"scalar-r2-13-source-terminal-assembly","locator":"Evolution/IntegralPrimitiveTerminal.lean:37–79; IsDiffusion.parisi_value_assembly; application in ContactAssembly.lean:41–42,54–55,70","statement":"For every minimizer gamma and supplied diffusion X on W, there exists U with the stopped-gradient martingale, almost-sure |U|≤1, U²=1 and full t→1− gradient convergence, mean-square convergence to U, parisi W gamma = E|X_1|−∫₀¹t gamma(t)dt = E[U B_1] = ∫₀¹E curvature(t,X_t)dt, interval integrability, and cutoff convergence of that integral to parisi W gamma.","assumption_ids":["assumptions.value_minimizer","assumptions.value_diffusion"],"evidence_ids":[],"citation_ids":["scalar-r2-13-cite-terminal-parisi-assembly"],"boundary":"This applied helper identifies the Parisi value before any substitution of groundStateValue. Its own declaration explicitly assumes strip regularity, gradient contact, and the unit curvature moment, supplied by the recorded application. The penalty here is the full integral. Authored correspondence to inspected frozen source; unverified status is retained. No fresh Lean build, elaborated dependency extraction, axiom audit, Comparator acceptance, or independent mathematical acceptance is claimed."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_terminal_parisi_assembly","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/69473417dc487cafa5e2e39b3168d2c4a8bffdad2e91b6e486de48f122dda071","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-expectation-limit","anchor":"gaussian-expectation-limit","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-expectation-limit","anchor":"gaussian-expectation-limit","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"gaussian-expectation-limit\"></a>\n\n#### 10.2.4 Passing the expectation bound and returning to the value\n\nSince $S^2=1$, $\\|\\widehat S_n\\|=1$ for $n>0$, and every residual\nprojection contracts. The column formula gives\n$|E_{n,j}|\\le1+\\|\\widehat z_{j+1,n}\\|^2$.\nDefine\n$$\nc_n=\\sum_{j<K}(1+\\|\\widehat z_{j+1,n}\\|^2).\n$$\nThen $\\sum_jE_{n,j}+c_n\\ge0$, $\\mathbb E c_n=2K$ exactly, and\n$c_n\\to2K$ almost surely. Fatou's inequality, the finite bound and\n$e_n\\to P_*$ imply\n$$\n\\sum_{j<K}a_jb_{j+1}+2K\n\\le\\liminf_n\\mathbb E\\bigl[\\sum_{j<K}E_{n,j}+c_n\\bigr]\n\\le P_*+2K.\n$$\nCancelling $2K$ proves the claimed predictable-coefficient inequality.\nThe source's constant-limit helper establishes the same nonnegative\ncomparison by clipping at the limiting constant and applying dominated\nconvergence. It shifts to dimensions $n+1$ to avoid dimension zero.\nIt does not apply Fatou directly to the signed energies or presume\nuniform integrability. [Column bound and correction][scalar-empirical-bound];\n[expectation limit and final theorem][scalar-predictable].\n\nFor the Euler construction in §7, take $K=N$, relabel its $Z_{j+1}$ as\n$z_j$, and set $f_j=g_{j+1}$. Each normalized increment depends only\non $z_0,\\ldots,z_j$ and the increments are orthonormal when their raw\nsecond moments are positive. The rounded sign is spin-valued almost\nsurely: its tie event is null by the independent last coordinate.\nThe scalar implementation uses a measurable Bool spin equal to that\nsign almost surely. It follows that\n$$\nS_{T,N}=\\sum_{j=1}^N A_jB_{j+1}\\le P_*.\n$$\nThis finite inequality assumes admissibility, $0\\le T<1$, $N>0$ and\npositive active second moments, but no minimizer or diffusion.\n[Literal scalar specialization][scalar-literal-lower].\n\nAt a minimizer, the separate contact and diffusion arguments establish\nthe fixed-strip inputs and eventual positivity. First take the mesh\nlimit to obtain $I(T)\\le P_*$, then let $T\\uparrow1$. The scalar\nterminal assembly identifies that last limit with $\\mathcal P(\\gamma)$;\ntogether with $P_*\\le\\mathcal P(\\gamma)$ this proves equality.\nThe third scalar endpoint constructs its own diffusion before making\nthis application. These sources therefore supply an independent route\nto the equality, alongside the imported Parisi formula in §6.\n[Contact assembly][scalar-contact-assembly]. The inspection here does\nnot change the research replay outcome in §13.\n","span":{"start_byte":178219,"end_byte":180612,"start_line":3807,"end_line":3858},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=gaussian-expectation-limit","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=gaussian-expectation-limit"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_terminal_parisi_assembly&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_terminal_parisi_assembly&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&node=scalar.source_terminal_parisi_assembly&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&node=scalar.source_terminal_parisi_assembly&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"field:953df07698adfd24c9fa6ccc9980a73eeb4d940cd2921aaa0d2e3b8094412d9e","from":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_terminal_parisi_assembly","to":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/assumptions.value_diffusion","kind":"assumption_ids","semantic_class":"explicit_assumption_reference","derived":true,"owner_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_terminal_parisi_assembly","source_field":"assumption_ids"},{"family":"arguments","id":"field:a5beebb38445926cc7afac37d905d5b4c372dd20c2f969da7c4a8367bcb0ea20","from":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_terminal_parisi_assembly","to":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/assumptions.value_m